---
import Docs from "~/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { Code } from "astro:components";
---

<Docs
  title="Telemetry"
  description="Stack's opt-in anonymous usage telemetry: off by default, no default endpoint, what an event contains and how to turn it off."
  eyebrow="Reference"
>
  <p>
    This page describes the client source in{" "}
    <code>packages/core/src/telemetry.ts</code> and the CLI entry point. It is
    not a statement about any hosted endpoint.
  </p>

  <h2>Default: off</h2>
  <p>
    Telemetry stays off until you opt in with{" "}
    <code>stack telemetry enable</code>, which saves the choice to{" "}
    <code>~/.ashlr/stack/config.json</code>. The CLI does not prompt for it.
    Core also exports a first-run prompt that defaults to <strong>N</strong>{" "}
    and records "disabled" when there is no terminal, but the CLI does not call
    it in this source.
  </p>
  <p>
    Opting in is not enough to send anything. The source has no default
    endpoint: an event is posted only when <code>STACK_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT</code>{" "}
    or a saved <code>endpoint</code> in the config file names one. Without
    either, the emit call returns without sending.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>STACK_TELEMETRY=0</code> turns it off for that process whatever the
    saved choice is. <code>STACK_TELEMETRY=1</code> does not opt you in.
  </p>

  <h2>What's collected</h2>
  <p>
    When you have opted in and an endpoint is set, each CLI run posts one event
    with these fields, with a one-second timeout and errors ignored:
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 28%">Field</th>
        <th>Value</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>type</code></td>
        <td>Always <code>"command"</code> from the CLI. The core API also accepts <code>"error"</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>command</code></td>
        <td>The first command-line argument, usually the subcommand (<code>"add"</code>, <code>"scan"</code>). If the first argument is a flag such as <code>--version</code>, that string is sent. Later arguments are not sent.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>exitCode</code></td>
        <td>The process exit code. <code>0</code> = success.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>durationMs</code></td>
        <td>Milliseconds from CLI start until the command finished.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>installId</code></td>
        <td>A random UUID stored in <code>~/.ashlr/stack/config.json</code>. It links events from the same installation.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>runId</code></td>
        <td>A fresh random UUID per process. Not stored.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>stackVersion</code></td>
        <td>A constant in the telemetry module. It is not read from the installed package, so it may not match <code>stack --version</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>platform</code></td>
        <td>The Node/Bun <code>process.platform</code> value, such as <code>darwin</code>, <code>linux</code> or <code>win32</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What's never collected</h2>
  <p>The fixed payload has no field for:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Working directory, filesystem paths, or project names.</li>
    <li>Providers configured for a project or <code>.stack.toml</code> / <code>.stack.local.toml</code> contents.</li>
    <li>Secret values, API keys, or anything stored in Phantom.</li>
    <li>Environment variable values.</li>
    <li>Email address or any account identifier.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Any endpoint you configure also sees network metadata such as your IP
    address. Stack's source does not control what that server logs or keeps;
    check its policy before you set an endpoint.
  </p>

  <h2>How to opt out</h2>

  <Code
    code={`# Save the disabled choice to ~/.ashlr/stack/config.json
stack telemetry disable

# Or force it off for one process, whatever the saved choice
STACK_TELEMETRY=0 stack add supabase

# Or force it off in every shell
echo 'export STACK_TELEMETRY=0' >> ~/.zshrc`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Check or change the saved choice from the CLI. Bare{" "}
    <code>stack telemetry</code> runs <code>status</code>:
  </p>

  <Code
    code={`stack telemetry status   # prints enabled or disabled
stack telemetry enable   # opt in
stack telemetry disable  # opt out`}
    lang="bash"
    theme="github-dark-dimmed"
  />

  <p>
    Opting out sends nothing, not even an opt-out event.
  </p>

  <h2>CI environments</h2>
  <p>
    CI behaves like any other environment: telemetry is off unless that
    machine's config file records an opt-in, and nothing is sent without an
    endpoint. Set <code>STACK_TELEMETRY=0</code> to rule it out regardless of
    the config. The update check, a separate request to the npm registry, is
    skipped when <code>CI</code> is <code>1</code> or <code>true</code>, or
    when <code>STACK_NO_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> is set.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    For the field list, opt-out options and recorded retention targets, see
    the <a href="/docs/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </div>
</Docs>
